\chapter{Your first native executable}
\label{ch:4}
\index{Your first native executable}

The example implements Fibonacci by recursion and verifies one result with an assertion. The recursive call has the same explicit signature as the initial call. The local binding in \OnxCodeTerm{main} gives the result a name, and a zero process result signals success. This program deliberately uses the core language and console intrinsics, so it is a good starting point for diagnosing a new compiler installation.

\OnxInputCode[]{../../on1x/examples/hello.on1x}
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
build/on1x/on1xc --check on1x/examples/hello.on1x
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-build.py on1x/examples/hello.on1x \
  --on1xc build/on1x/on1xc -o build/on1x-hello
build/on1x-hello
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

The first command checks the source. The helper runs emission, selects the required runtime sources and kernel groups, and invokes the host toolchain. Running the resulting binary prints 21. Change the argument from 8 to 7 and update the assertion to 13 to distinguish successful recompilation from accidentally running an older binary. Keep the assertion when experimenting with recursion.

\section{Three results from the same run}
Start with a call small enough to work out in the margin.
For input 3, the left recursive call asks for the value at 2, which is
the sum of the values at 1 and 0. Those calls return their arguments,
so the left call returns 1. The right call for the original input asks
for the value at 1 and returns 1 as well. The original call returns 2;
none of these intermediate returns prints anything. Extending the same
recurrence gives 3, 5, 8, 13 and 21 at indices 4 through 8.

There are three separate observations here. The value returned by
\OnxCodeTerm{fib} is bound inside the program. The line of output comes
from passing that value to the console. The integer returned by
\OnxCodeTerm{main} is the process result. A shell script testing for a
successful exit should expect zero rather than 21. Conversely, a script
testing the computation should check the printed value or leave the
assertion in place; a zero exit alone need not describe that computation.

Try changing just the expected value in the assertion. This remains a
well-typed comparison, so checking the source should succeed. Running the
new executable should fail the assertion. That contrast gives a quick way
to tell a source diagnostic from an error reached by executing checked code.

